**Read:** [Reliability-centered maintenance — NASA, September 2008](../sources/Reliability-Centered-Maintenance-NASA.lingua#r_08ZD3ATSYCJX5586EYSY0M4N3R), functional analysis, and the shared supply-dependence Record.

For a stated demand, suppose a common event disables both supplies with probability qc. Conditional on its absence, each supply fails independently with probability q. Joint failure is qc+(1−qc)q². For hypothetical q=0.01 and qc=0.002, it is 0.0020998, compared with 0.0001 in the model that omits the common event.

The equation does not infer qc from a diagram. Two tanks may depend on one regulator; two compressors may share cooling, power, controls or maintenance errors. Standby changeover may also fail or take longer than the useful reserve permits.

**Work:** Define the demand and conditional events before calculating. Draw the actual dependencies of two proposed reserve arrangements. Add a switching failure and a finite reserve, keeping overlapping events from being counted twice. Identify evidence required to estimate each probability.

**Return:** [Industrial gases, vacuum and high-purity supplies — from molecules to useful service](../Industrial-gases-and-vacuum.lingua#r_3GHQJEYMYEN6JDKSF4PPJ403RF).
